{"name":"Elemental Grey","hex":"#a0a09f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"elemental","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or being an element (as opposed to a compound).","Basic, fundamental or elementary."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A creature (usually a spirit) that is attuned with, or composed of, one of the classical elements: air, earth, fire and water or variations of them like ice, lightning, etc. They sometimes have unique proper names and sometimes are referred to as Air, Earth, Fire, or Water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elemental"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elemental","blocks":[{"text":"An elemental is a mythic supernatural being that is described in occult and alchemical works from around the time of the European Renaissance, and particularly elaborated in the 16th century works of Paracelsus. According to Paracelsus and his subsequent followers, there are four categories of elementals, which are gnomes, undines, sylphs, and salamanders. These correspond to the four Empedoclean elements of antiquity: earth, water, air, and fire, respectively. Terms employed for beings associated with alchemical elements vary by source and gloss."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Undine_Rising_from_the_Waters%2C_front.JPG/330px-Undine_Rising_from_the_Waters%2C_front.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elemental","wikidata":"Q1786347"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG/500px-The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG/1280px-The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG/500px-The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rococo set of personification figurines of the Four Elements, 1760s, Chelsea porcelain","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Grey_Longhaired_Female_Cat.jpg/330px-Grey_Longhaired_Female_Cat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Grey_Longhaired_Female_Cat.jpg/330px-Grey_Longhaired_Female_Cat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","l